      "proposed_laureates": ["Hidetoshi Katori", "Jun Ye"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks combine a profound advance in precision measurement with new ways to test fundamental physics. The achievement is exceptionally strong.",
        "attribution": "Katori and Ye made complementary, independently important contributions to the optical lattice clock concept and its realization.",
        "maturity": "The clocks have been developed and refined over many years, with repeated demonstrations of extraordinary precision.",
        "uncertainties": ["The boundary between the clock invention and broader advances in optical frequency metrology merits careful credit."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B036",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Immanuel Bloch", "Jean Dalibard", "Markus Greiner"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Ultracold atoms in optical lattices made controlled studies of quantum many-body physics possible, including direct exploration of models that are difficult to solve or realize otherwise.",
        "attribution": "Bloch, Greiner, and Dalibard represent central experimental and theoretical contributions to establishing this field; the work also has a wider community of contributors.",
        "maturity": "The experiments have been reproduced and extended across many systems, establishing a durable research program.",
        "uncertainties": ["The nominated contributions span a broad field, so the most prize-worthy shared achievement and its attribution need definition."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B008",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Alexei Kitaev", "Andrew M. Steane", "Peter W. Shor"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Quantum error correction showed how quantum information can be protected despite noise, turning fault-tolerant quantum computation from an apparent impossibility into a physical goal.",
        "attribution": "Shor and Steane introduced landmark codes, while Kitaev developed influential topological approaches. Their distinct theoretical contributions are substantial.",
        "maturity": "The principles are well established and have guided increasingly capable experimental demonstrations, though large-scale fault tolerance remains in progress.",
        "uncertainties": ["The eventual physical implementations may bring additional contributors into focus."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B004",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Peter F. Moulton", "Ursula Keller", "Wilson Sibbett"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Practical femtosecond laser sources enabled the study and control of ultrafast processes, including the attosecond science that reveals electron dynamics in atoms.",
        "attribution": "The proposed names capture important contributions to titanium-sapphire lasers and passive mode-locking, but the technological development involved multiple related advances.",
        "maturity": "The sources and techniques have been used and improved for decades, with clear impact across ultrafast science.",
        "uncertainties": ["The relative credit for the laser material, mode-locking methods, and practical femtosecond sources is not simple."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B034",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Antoine Browaeys", "Mark Saffman", "Mikhail D. Lukin"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Rydberg blockade and programmable neutral-atom arrays offer an unusually direct and flexible route to controlled quantum many-body experiments and computation.",
        "attribution": "Browaeys, Saffman, and Lukin have major contributions to the experimental platforms and their underlying ideas. The field's development is broad.",
        "maturity": "Arrays have grown rapidly in size and capability, and multiple groups have demonstrated control; the most consequential scientific uses are still emerging.",
        "uncertainties": ["The field is young, and it remains uncertain which results will prove most foundational."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B001",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Charles L. Bennett", "David N. Spergel", "Lyman A. Page Jr."],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "WMAP's precision mapping of the cosmic microwave background transformed cosmology into a quantitatively constrained field and tested the standard picture of cosmic evolution.",
        "attribution": "Bennett and Page led central experimental work, while Spergel made important contributions to interpretation. The mission and science were collective.",
        "maturity": "The measurements have been extensively analyzed and compared with later observations, and their impact is well established.",
        "uncertainties": ["The distinction between WMAP's specific contribution and the wider sequence of CMB measurements, including COBE and Planck, needs careful treatment."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B050",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Christian J. Bordé", "David E. Pritchard", "Mark A. Kasevich"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Atom interferometry brought matter-wave interference into precision measurement, enabling sensitive tests of gravity and other fundamental quantities.",
        "attribution": "Bordé, Pritchard, and Kasevich represent important conceptual and experimental steps, although the technique has several branches and contributors.",
        "maturity": "The method has been tested and refined over decades and is now a well-established precision tool.",
        "uncertainties": ["The nominated scope combines developments across different interferometer designs and applications."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B020",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Juan Ignacio Cirac", "Peter Zoller", "Rainer Blatt"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Trapped ions provided one of the clearest demonstrations that quantum logic can be implemented and controlled at the level of individual particles.",
        "attribution": "Cirac and Zoller supplied influential theoretical foundations, while Blatt's group achieved landmark experimental realizations.",
        "maturity": "The platform has produced repeated, precise demonstrations and continues to advance, though scalable fault-tolerant computation is not yet established.",
        "uncertainties": ["The proposed discovery overlaps with broader quantum information advances and the separate theoretical contribution listed for trapped-ion computing and simulation."]
      }
    }
  ]
